        (323, "In an isobilateral leaf, what prevents the parallel veins from fusing with one another along their length?",
         {"A": "They run longitudinally in parallel lines without intersecting, connected only by minute transverse anastomosing veinlets", "B": "They are separated by thick cork layers", "C": "They are located in separate leaves", "D": "They repel each other with magnetic charges"},
         "A", "Parallel Venation Architecture.",
         "1. Parallel venation consists of parallel primary longitudinal veins that run from leaf base to apex without converging, interconnected by fine perpendicular commissural veinlets that ensure redundancy in water supply.",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Parallel Vein Architecture"),

        (324, "What histological feature distinguishes the hypodermis of a dicot stem from that of a monocot stem under a light microscope?",
         {"A": "Dicot stem hypodermis stains for pectin/cellulose with uneven corner thickenings (collenchyma); monocot stem hypodermis stains red with phloroglucinol for lignin (sclerenchyma)", "B": "Dicot stem has sclerenchyma while monocot stem has collenchyma", "C": "Both have identical thin-walled parenchyma", "D": "Dicot stem lacks a hypodermis entirely"},
         "A", "Hypodermis Staining Dicot vs Monocot.",
         "1. Dicot stem hypodermis is collenchymatous (living, cellulosic/pectic corner thickenings).\n2. Monocot stem hypodermis is sclerenchymatous (dead, heavily lignified, staining bright red/magenta with phloroglucinol-HCl).",
         "Option B reverses the tissue types.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Hypodermis Staining Dicot vs Monocot"),

        (325, "In a dicot stem, what constitutes the 'vascular cylinder' before secondary growth begins?",
         {"A": "A discrete ring of conjoint collateral open vascular bundles separated by primary medullary rays", "B": "A continuous solid ring of wood without rays", "C": "A scattered mass of closed bundles", "D": "A central core of primary xylem surrounded by phloem"},
         "A", "Primary Vascular Ring in Dicot Stem.",
         "1. In the primary state of a dicot stem, the vascular cylinder consists of separate, open vascular bundles arranged in a neat ring, punctuated by intervening radial bands of parenchymatous medullary rays.",
         "Option B describes a secondary woody trunk. Option C is a monocot stem.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Primary Vascular Cylinder"),

        (326, "Why are root hairs strictly unicellular while stem trichomes are predominantly multicellular?",
         {"A": "Root hairs are direct tubular outgrowths of individual epiblema cells without cell division; trichomes arise by repeated mitotic divisions of an epidermal initial", "B": "Root hairs have nuclei while trichomes have no DNA", "C": "Root hairs are made of wood while trichomes are made of cutin", "D": "Trichomes absorb water while root hairs prevent transpiration"},
         "A", "Developmental Origin of Root Hairs vs Trichomes.",
         "1. A root hair is a simple, unbranched tubular extension of a single rhizodermal (trichoblast) cell.\n2. Stem trichomes originate from an epidermal initial cell that undergoes multiple transverse and longitudinal mitotic divisions, forming multicellular stalks and heads.",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Trichome vs Root Hair Development"),

        (327, "In which plant tissue does the cell wall have the highest percentage of pectin and hemicellulose relative to cellulose?",
         {"A": "Collenchyma", "B": "Sclerenchyma fibres", "C": "Xylem tracheids", "D": "Cork cells"},
         "A", "Pectin Content in Collenchyma.",
         "1. Collenchyma cell walls contain exceptionally high levels of pectin (up to 45%) and hemicellulose (up to 35%), with cellulose making up only 20-30%, conferring high water content and plastic flexibility.",
         "Sclerenchyma and tracheids are dominated by cellulose and lignin. Cork has suberin.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Collenchyma Pectin Proportion"),

        (328, "What is the function of the waxy cuticle on the leaf epidermis during intense solar heat?",
         {"A": "Reflects excess harmful UV radiation and provides a hydrophobic barrier that reduces non-stomatal cuticular transpiration to under 5-10% of total water loss", "B": "Absorbs water vapor directly from dry air", "C": "Conducts heat into the mesophyll to cook enzymes", "D": "Dissolves stomata at noon"},
         "A", "Cuticular Function in Solar Heat.",
         "1. The cuticle is a complex multi-layered polyester of cutin embedded in epicuticular waxes.\n2. It reflects damaging UV rays and acts as an impermeable seal, restricting water loss almost entirely to controllable stomatal apertures.",
         "Options B, C, and D are absurd.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Cuticle Biomechanics"),

        (329, "In an anatomical investigation of a leaf, a student notices that the stomata open during the night and close during the day. This plant is most likely a:",
         {"A": "CAM plant (Crassulacean Acid Metabolism, e.g., Opuntia, pineapple)", "B": "C3 plant (e.g., wheat)", "C": "C4 plant (e.g., maize)", "D": "Submerged hydrophyte"},
         "A", "Scotactive Stomata in CAM Plants.",
         "1. Desert succulents (CAM plants: Opuntia, Kalanchoe, pineapple) exhibit scotactive stomata (open at night to fix CO2 into malic acid; closed during scorching daylight to conserve water).",
         "C3 and C4 plants have photoactive stomata (open during the day).",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Scotactive Stomata"),

        (330, "What is the Casparian strip composed of, and what does it stain with?",
         {"A": "Suberin (and lignin); stains red/brown with Sudan dyes and phloroglucinol", "B": "Cellulose; stains blue with iodine", "C": "Pure starch; stains yellow with water", "D": "Chlorophyll; stains green with alcohol"},
         "A", "Casparian Strip Chemistry and Staining.",
         "1. Casparian strips are composed of the hydrophobic macromolecule suberin, often cross-linked with lignin.\n2. They stain selectively with lipophilic dyes like Sudan III/IV or Sudan Black, and give a positive lignin reaction with acidified phloroglucinol.",
         "Options B, C, and D are incorrect.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Casparian Strip Chemistry"),

        (331, "In the root pericycle of a dicot, the cells located directly outside the protoxylem give rise to:",
         {"A": "Part of the vascular cambium and lateral roots", "B": "Root hairs", "C": "Root caps", "D": "Phelloderm"},
         "A", "Pericycle Above Protoxylem.",
         "1. In a dicot root, pericycle cells lying directly outside the protoxylem poles dedifferentiate to complete the vascular cambium ring and initiate lateral root primordia.",
         "Root hairs develop from epiblema.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Pericycle Outside Protoxylem"),

        (332, "In which plant group do we find 'amphistomatic' leaves with stomata on both upper and lower epidermises?",
         {"A": "Monocotyledons (isobilateral leaves of grasses and cereals)", "B": "Submerged hydrophytes", "C": "Floating hydrophytes", "D": "Shade-loving forest floor herbs"},
         "A", "Amphistomatic Leaves in Monocots.",
         "1. Amphistomatic leaves possess stomata on both the upper and lower surfaces in nearly equal numbers, characteristic of isobilateral monocot leaves (wheat, maize, grasses).",
         "Floating hydrophytes are epistomatic (upper only). Submerged are astomatic.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Amphistomatic Leaves Monocots"),

        (333, "Why are monocot leaves described as 'isobilateral'?",
         {"A": "Both upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces are structurally similar, receiving comparable light and having undifferentiated mesophyll", "B": "They can be divided into two bilateral halves by any radial plane", "C": "They produce two seeds per leaf", "D": "They have two midribs"},
         "A", "Isobilateral Leaf Meaning.",
         "1. 'Isobilateral' means having equal/similar sides: the internal anatomy (uniform mesophyll, equal stomatal frequency, symmetrical cuticle) is structurally equivalent on both surfaces.",
         "Option B describes actinomorphy in flowers.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Isobilateral Leaf Meaning"),

        (334, "What anatomical feature of dicot leaves causes reticulate venation to be visible as an intricate network on the leaf blade?",
         {"A": "Repeated branching of lateral veins into secondary, tertiary veins, and minute interconnected veinlets ending freely in areoles", "B": "Parallel unbranched lines running from base to tip", "C": "Continuous spiral rings of wood", "D": "Absence of phloem in veins"},
         "A", "Reticulate Venation Anatomical Basis.",
         "1. Reticulate venation results from repeated divergent branching of lateral veins into finer veinlets that anastomose to form a polygonal meshwork, enclosing small islands of mesophyll called areoles with terminal vein endings.",
         "Option B describes parallel venation.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Reticulate Venation Anatomy"),
